1
00:00:00,620 --> 00:00:05,400
The last of the microcytic anemias would be due to thalassemia.

2
00:00:05,940 --> 00:00:09,140
Thalassemia is due to a decreased synthesis of globin chains.

3
00:00:09,380 --> 00:00:11,660
That's what we find in a thalassemia,

4
00:00:11,780 --> 00:00:14,360
that there's going to be decreased production of globin chains.

5
00:00:14,840 --> 00:00:17,100
And so if there's decreased production of globin chains,

6
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:20,020
there's going to be decreased hemoglobin,

7
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:22,600
because globin would be down, so hemoglobin would be down,

8
00:00:22,860 --> 00:00:26,620
and decreased hemoglobin would then result in a microcytic anemia

9
00:00:26,620 --> 00:00:29,300
as per the paradigm that we've established.

10
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:33,080
So that's the general idea behind thalassemia.

11
00:00:33,260 --> 00:00:35,680
I'd like to just stop here for a second

12
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:39,140
and remind you of a little bit of a technicality.

13
00:00:39,460 --> 00:00:41,920
Notice that I'm saying that we're talking about

14
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:44,140
a decrease in synthesis of the globin chain.

15
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:48,360
So a decrease in synthesis or production of the amount of globin chain

16
00:00:48,360 --> 00:00:49,360
is called thalassemia,

17
00:00:49,820 --> 00:00:51,580
whereas you could have some diseases

18
00:00:51,580 --> 00:00:56,100
in which there is an abnormality of the globin chain

19
00:00:56,100 --> 00:00:59,240
after it's produced, for example, like in sickle cell anemia.

20
00:00:59,660 --> 00:01:01,980
So in sickle cell anemia, you're producing globin chain.

21
00:01:02,140 --> 00:01:03,940
However, the globin chain is defective.

22
00:01:04,360 --> 00:01:07,400
So in thalassemia, the key is a decrease in synthesis

23
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:09,220
of the globin chain.

24
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:13,200
Thalassemia is usually due to an inherited mutation.

25
00:01:13,740 --> 00:01:15,820
Prevalence of thalassemia is explained

26
00:01:15,820 --> 00:01:18,760
by the fact that carriers are protected

27
00:01:18,760 --> 00:01:21,080
against plasmodium falciparum malaria.

28
00:01:21,420 --> 00:01:24,000
It's not enough to know that they're protected against malaria.

29
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:25,580
You need to know that they're protected

30
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:28,600
against plasmodium falciparum malaria.

31
00:01:29,380 --> 00:01:33,240
Thalassemia is divided into alpha and beta thalassemia.

32
00:01:33,720 --> 00:01:36,000
And so if there's a decrease in production of alpha chains,

33
00:01:36,060 --> 00:01:37,600
we call that alpha thalassemia.

34
00:01:38,040 --> 00:01:40,400
And if there's a decrease in production of beta chains,

35
00:01:40,880 --> 00:01:42,820
then we call that beta thalassemia.

36
00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:45,920
It's worth taking a moment here to remind ourselves

37
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:47,700
of the normal types of hemoglobin.

38
00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:51,320
So the three normal types of hemoglobin usually seen

39
00:01:51,320 --> 00:01:54,540
in a human being are fetal hemoglobin,

40
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:59,520
which is alpha 2, gamma 2, hemoglobin A,

41
00:01:59,740 --> 00:02:04,240
which is alpha 2, beta 2, and then hemoglobin A2,

42
00:02:04,420 --> 00:02:06,440
which is alpha 2, delta 2.

43
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:08,700
Of all of these hemoglobins, you can see

44
00:02:08,700 --> 00:02:10,920
that there's a consistent use of alpha.

45
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:14,680
In one way, you can think of it as sort of the most important

46
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:17,520
of all of the globin chains.

47
00:02:17,940 --> 00:02:19,980
And alpha is used by everybody.

48
00:02:20,920 --> 00:02:23,760
Now I use that to remind me that there are actually

49
00:02:23,760 --> 00:02:25,780
four copies of the alpha gene,

50
00:02:26,060 --> 00:02:28,120
i.e. alpha is particularly important,

51
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:30,380
and so therefore there's a couple extra copies

52
00:02:30,380 --> 00:02:31,640
of the alpha gene.

53
00:02:32,020 --> 00:02:35,540
And so when you think about the genetics of the alpha chain,

54
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:39,020
it's important to know that the alpha chain,

55
00:02:39,120 --> 00:02:40,860
that there are normally four alpha alleles

56
00:02:40,860 --> 00:02:42,760
and that they're present on chromosome 16.

57
00:02:42,760 --> 00:02:45,020
So basically this is what it looks like.

58
00:02:45,140 --> 00:02:47,160
This is one copy of chromosome 16

59
00:02:47,160 --> 00:02:48,500
and there'd be two genes here,

60
00:02:48,620 --> 00:02:50,640
and then one copy of chromosome 16

61
00:02:50,640 --> 00:02:51,920
and there'd be two genes here.

62
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:54,500
So it's important to know that there are four alleles

63
00:02:54,500 --> 00:02:57,680
in alpha, four normal alpha alleles.

64
00:02:58,100 --> 00:02:59,300
Now in alpha thalassemia,

65
00:02:59,440 --> 00:03:01,380
the problem is going to be gene deletion.

66
00:03:01,580 --> 00:03:02,860
This is also important to know.

67
00:03:02,980 --> 00:03:05,980
You need to know that in alpha thalassemia,

68
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:07,420
the problem is gene deletion.

69
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:10,520
So what's going to happen is we're going to knock out genes,

70
00:03:10,520 --> 00:03:13,400
and the knockout of the gene is then going to result

71
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:14,700
in clinical findings.

72
00:03:15,320 --> 00:03:17,860
Now the first possibility would be that one gene is deleted.

73
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:20,980
So imagine again that we've got two copies of alpha

74
00:03:20,980 --> 00:03:23,780
on this one chromosome and two alleles here

75
00:03:23,780 --> 00:03:25,020
on the other chromosome.

76
00:03:25,160 --> 00:03:27,040
If we knock out one alpha,

77
00:03:27,480 --> 00:03:28,840
the patients are asymptomatic.

78
00:03:29,320 --> 00:03:32,760
So that makes it easy because we don't have to worry

79
00:03:32,760 --> 00:03:34,680
about one gene deleted.

80
00:03:35,580 --> 00:03:37,460
Now what about if we knock out two genes?

81
00:03:37,460 --> 00:03:40,300
Well, if we knock out two genes, for example, here and here,

82
00:03:40,780 --> 00:03:42,480
and then here and here,

83
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:44,320
if we knock out two genes,

84
00:03:44,820 --> 00:03:47,120
then the patients develop a mild anemia

85
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:50,080
with a slightly increased red blood cell count.

86
00:03:50,360 --> 00:03:51,580
So there'll be a mild anemia

87
00:03:51,580 --> 00:03:53,760
with a slightly increased red blood cell count.

88
00:03:54,260 --> 00:03:58,580
Not something that you have to worry about too much clinically.

89
00:03:58,780 --> 00:04:00,260
Sometimes these patients don't even know

90
00:04:00,260 --> 00:04:02,700
that they have a alpha thalassemia,

91
00:04:02,840 --> 00:04:05,240
and not something that you have to worry too much about

92
00:04:05,240 --> 00:04:06,140
on exams either.

93
00:04:06,140 --> 00:04:09,320
But what is important for examination purposes,

94
00:04:09,740 --> 00:04:11,020
and somewhat clinically,

95
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:13,140
is that really there's two possibilities

96
00:04:13,140 --> 00:04:14,760
when you knock out two genes.

97
00:04:14,900 --> 00:04:17,380
So it's possible that you could knock out both genes

98
00:04:17,380 --> 00:04:19,040
on the same chromosome.

99
00:04:19,420 --> 00:04:22,560
For example, both of them are here on this same chromosome.

100
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:25,660
We name that a cis deletion.

101
00:04:25,780 --> 00:04:26,420
Cis means width.

102
00:04:26,780 --> 00:04:28,560
Or it's possible that you could knock out

103
00:04:28,560 --> 00:04:31,000
these on opposite chromosomes,

104
00:04:31,320 --> 00:04:32,720
and that's called trans.

105
00:04:32,720 --> 00:04:34,760
One possibility is cis,

106
00:04:34,820 --> 00:04:36,160
and the other possibility is trans.

107
00:04:36,280 --> 00:04:38,760
And the cis deletion is important

108
00:04:38,760 --> 00:04:41,820
because the cis deletion is worse than the trans deletion.

109
00:04:41,880 --> 00:04:43,880
So that's a question that could be asked.

110
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:45,540
Which is worse, the cis or the trans?

111
00:04:45,780 --> 00:04:48,060
And the reason that the cis is worse

112
00:04:48,060 --> 00:04:51,040
is because it's associated with an increased risk

113
00:04:51,040 --> 00:04:52,920
of severe thalassemia in the offspring,

114
00:04:53,520 --> 00:04:55,820
i.e. if a person has two genes deleted,

115
00:04:55,960 --> 00:04:56,600
like this,

116
00:04:57,100 --> 00:05:00,320
then it's possible that when they pass this chromosome,

117
00:05:00,520 --> 00:05:02,920
they could pass both deleted copies.

118
00:05:03,300 --> 00:05:05,980
And so if that person happens to receive

119
00:05:05,980 --> 00:05:09,080
another bad copy from, let's say, a parent,

120
00:05:09,360 --> 00:05:10,940
that would then result in the risk

121
00:05:10,940 --> 00:05:12,660
for severe anemia in offspring.

122
00:05:13,080 --> 00:05:15,420
So that's one important feature of the cis deletion.

123
00:05:15,760 --> 00:05:17,580
The second important feature of the cis deletion

124
00:05:17,580 --> 00:05:19,400
is that it's more commonly seen in Asians.

125
00:05:19,940 --> 00:05:21,320
And some people say that

126
00:05:21,320 --> 00:05:24,580
the higher rate of spontaneous abortions seen in Asia,

127
00:05:25,200 --> 00:05:26,660
I guess one point to be aware of

128
00:05:26,660 --> 00:05:27,620
is that in Asia,

129
00:05:27,740 --> 00:05:29,780
there's a higher rate of spontaneous abortion,

130
00:05:30,220 --> 00:05:31,400
and some believe that

131
00:05:31,400 --> 00:05:33,900
it's actually due to the fact

132
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:36,840
that cis deletions are more common in Asia.

133
00:05:37,160 --> 00:05:37,620
And by the way,

134
00:05:37,660 --> 00:05:39,920
the trans deletions are more common in Africa.

135
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:42,020
Cis deletions are more common in Asia,

136
00:05:42,480 --> 00:05:44,620
trans deletions are more common in Africa.

137
00:05:45,620 --> 00:05:47,340
Patients who have three genes deleted,

138
00:05:47,720 --> 00:05:49,380
they get a severe anemia.

139
00:05:50,040 --> 00:05:51,840
Again, if you're going to knock out three genes,

140
00:05:51,900 --> 00:05:53,120
you really don't have much choice.

141
00:05:53,200 --> 00:05:55,660
You've got to knock out two-on-one chromosome,

142
00:05:55,940 --> 00:05:57,920
and then the other one would be knocked out here.

143
00:05:58,140 --> 00:05:59,980
What's then going to happen is that

144
00:05:59,980 --> 00:06:03,440
as the patient develops in utero,

145
00:06:03,620 --> 00:06:05,940
there really is no clinically detected problem.

146
00:06:06,300 --> 00:06:07,860
Remember that the hemoglobin F,

147
00:06:07,940 --> 00:06:09,440
which was the fetal hemoglobin,

148
00:06:09,820 --> 00:06:13,840
is basically composed of alpha-2 and then gamma-2.

149
00:06:13,840 --> 00:06:17,600
And so even though only one alpha gene is being expressed,

150
00:06:18,020 --> 00:06:20,620
the presence of that one alpha gene

151
00:06:20,620 --> 00:06:23,020
is sufficient enough for the fetus

152
00:06:23,020 --> 00:06:24,660
to really avoid any problems.

153
00:06:25,200 --> 00:06:26,420
When the fetus is born

154
00:06:26,420 --> 00:06:29,140
and the patient begins to produce hemoglobin A

155
00:06:29,140 --> 00:06:30,100
and hemoglobin A2,

156
00:06:30,540 --> 00:06:33,340
that's going to increase the risk

157
00:06:33,340 --> 00:06:36,540
of forming tetramers of beta chains.

158
00:06:36,700 --> 00:06:38,200
Now, I guess to understand that,

159
00:06:38,260 --> 00:06:39,860
you have to know that normally what happens

160
00:06:39,860 --> 00:06:43,780
is sort of the alpha becomes a dimer

161
00:06:43,780 --> 00:06:46,060
and then the beta becomes a dimer.

162
00:06:46,360 --> 00:06:48,860
And so alpha-2 and beta-2 sort of join together

163
00:06:48,860 --> 00:06:51,340
and then that forms hemoglobin.

164
00:06:51,720 --> 00:06:54,440
However, if the patient doesn't have alpha,

165
00:06:54,660 --> 00:06:55,540
then what will happen is

166
00:06:55,540 --> 00:06:58,060
the betas will sort of join with another beta-2.

167
00:06:58,440 --> 00:07:00,280
So the beta-2 will join with beta-2

168
00:07:00,280 --> 00:07:02,600
and that would result in a tetramer of beta.

169
00:07:02,960 --> 00:07:04,620
And the problem with that tetramer of beta

170
00:07:04,620 --> 00:07:06,460
is that it damages the red blood cells.

171
00:07:06,960 --> 00:07:09,660
Now, a tetramer of beta is called hemoglobin H.

172
00:07:10,220 --> 00:07:11,240
That's important to know.

173
00:07:11,620 --> 00:07:14,080
And hemoglobin H would be seen on electrophoresis

174
00:07:14,080 --> 00:07:16,440
in alpha-thalassemia

175
00:07:16,440 --> 00:07:17,920
in which three genes are deleted.

176
00:07:18,540 --> 00:07:19,940
Now, if four genes are deleted,

177
00:07:20,080 --> 00:07:21,480
then you've got a major problem

178
00:07:21,480 --> 00:07:23,300
and it's going to express itself in the fetus

179
00:07:23,300 --> 00:07:25,420
because remember that hemoglobin F,

180
00:07:25,480 --> 00:07:26,960
which was the hemoglobin in the fetus,

181
00:07:27,240 --> 00:07:29,840
contains alpha-2 and gamma-2.

182
00:07:30,160 --> 00:07:31,420
And so if there's no alpha,

183
00:07:31,420 --> 00:07:34,860
then gamma-2 is going to join with another gamma-2

184
00:07:34,860 --> 00:07:37,600
and that's going to result in a tetramer of gamma

185
00:07:37,600 --> 00:07:39,180
and the tetramer of gamma

186
00:07:39,180 --> 00:07:41,700
would then damage the red blood cells.

187
00:07:41,780 --> 00:07:44,040
And these patients, they die in utero.

188
00:07:44,440 --> 00:07:46,280
And so this disease is lethal in utero.

189
00:07:46,600 --> 00:07:48,760
It's also called hydrops fetalis.

190
00:07:49,200 --> 00:07:52,480
Now, what would we see on hemoglobin electrophoresis?

191
00:07:52,560 --> 00:07:54,000
We'd see hemoglobin BARTs.

192
00:07:54,040 --> 00:07:55,280
And what is hemoglobin BARTs?

193
00:07:55,540 --> 00:07:57,900
It is a tetramer of gamma chains.

194
00:07:57,900 --> 00:08:01,960
So it represents tetramer of gamma chains.

195
00:08:02,380 --> 00:08:04,600
All right, so that's alpha-thalassemia.

196
00:08:04,960 --> 00:08:06,820
And now we go to beta-thalassemia.

197
00:08:06,900 --> 00:08:08,820
And what's important to know about beta-thalassemia,

198
00:08:08,920 --> 00:08:11,240
first of all, there are two beta genes.

199
00:08:11,380 --> 00:08:12,380
One is on chromosome

200
00:08:12,380 --> 00:08:15,980
and the other is on the second copy of the chromosome.

201
00:08:16,480 --> 00:08:17,660
So there are two beta genes

202
00:08:17,660 --> 00:08:19,500
and they're present on chromosome 11.

203
00:08:20,700 --> 00:08:22,880
And perhaps particularly important

204
00:08:22,880 --> 00:08:25,500
for this discussion and high yield

205
00:08:25,500 --> 00:08:28,800
is that beta-thalassemia is due to gene mutations,

206
00:08:29,140 --> 00:08:30,080
not deletions.

207
00:08:30,340 --> 00:08:33,240
Remember, alpha-thalassemia, gene deletions.

208
00:08:33,620 --> 00:08:35,560
Beta-thalassemia, gene mutations.

209
00:08:36,240 --> 00:08:38,200
Now, these mutations can create

210
00:08:38,200 --> 00:08:40,500
a variable production of beta chain.

211
00:08:40,900 --> 00:08:43,260
If the mutation results in complete knockout

212
00:08:43,260 --> 00:08:44,940
of the ability to produce beta chain,

213
00:08:45,240 --> 00:08:47,160
then we call that beta null.

214
00:08:47,720 --> 00:08:49,060
And if the mutation results

215
00:08:49,060 --> 00:08:51,020
in decreased production of beta chain,

216
00:08:51,020 --> 00:08:53,300
then we call that beta plus.

217
00:08:53,900 --> 00:08:55,240
So these are the two possibilities.

218
00:08:55,940 --> 00:08:58,260
Now, this creates a wide spectrum of disease.

219
00:08:58,560 --> 00:09:00,440
So, for example, a patient could have beta,

220
00:09:00,640 --> 00:09:02,320
a normal beta, and then beta plus.

221
00:09:02,540 --> 00:09:05,380
Or a patient could have beta plus, beta plus.

222
00:09:05,740 --> 00:09:08,460
Or a patient could have beta plus, beta null.

223
00:09:08,800 --> 00:09:11,140
Or a patient could have beta null, beta null.

224
00:09:11,340 --> 00:09:12,980
So there's a lot of permutations

225
00:09:12,980 --> 00:09:16,680
of beta plus and beta null that could exist.

226
00:09:16,900 --> 00:09:17,840
What we're going to do

227
00:09:17,840 --> 00:09:19,700
is we're just going to focus on the extremes.

228
00:09:19,700 --> 00:09:23,980
So one extreme, sort of the least severe extreme,

229
00:09:24,100 --> 00:09:25,540
the mildest form of disease,

230
00:09:25,800 --> 00:09:27,380
would be to have one normal beta,

231
00:09:27,780 --> 00:09:29,000
and to have one beta plus,

232
00:09:29,080 --> 00:09:30,560
where there was a slight decrease

233
00:09:30,560 --> 00:09:31,660
in the production of beta.

234
00:09:32,180 --> 00:09:34,640
This is called beta thalassemia minor.

235
00:09:35,640 --> 00:09:37,300
It's usually asymptomatic.

236
00:09:37,840 --> 00:09:39,000
And the patients may have

237
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:40,540
an increased red blood cell count.

238
00:09:40,940 --> 00:09:43,400
They usually have a microcytic hypochromic anemia

239
00:09:43,400 --> 00:09:45,080
with some target cells,

240
00:09:45,360 --> 00:09:47,240
but again, they are asymptomatic.

241
00:09:47,720 --> 00:09:49,600
Now, it's worth discussing for a moment here

242
00:09:49,600 --> 00:09:51,100
what target cells are,

243
00:09:51,180 --> 00:09:52,660
because this is the first time

244
00:09:52,660 --> 00:09:55,000
that we're encountering a target cell.

245
00:09:55,820 --> 00:09:57,500
So let's go back and remind ourselves

246
00:09:57,500 --> 00:09:58,960
that the normal red blood cell

247
00:09:58,960 --> 00:10:01,960
has a biconcave shape to it.

248
00:10:02,820 --> 00:10:05,740
And the biconcave shape is such that

249
00:10:05,740 --> 00:10:08,140
the edges here are thicker,

250
00:10:08,140 --> 00:10:10,960
and they contain hemoglobin, right?

251
00:10:10,960 --> 00:10:13,280
And the center is thinner,

252
00:10:13,480 --> 00:10:14,880
and it contains less hemoglobin.

253
00:10:15,280 --> 00:10:16,420
Now, when you look down

254
00:10:16,420 --> 00:10:18,000
at the red blood cell on the microscope,

255
00:10:18,240 --> 00:10:19,260
what you're actually seeing

256
00:10:19,260 --> 00:10:20,320
is the red blood cell

257
00:10:20,320 --> 00:10:22,220
with an area of central pallor.

258
00:10:22,380 --> 00:10:24,220
This represents the central pallor.

259
00:10:24,500 --> 00:10:25,480
And you see the edges,

260
00:10:25,700 --> 00:10:27,460
which are full of hemoglobin.

261
00:10:27,560 --> 00:10:29,180
So this is the normal image

262
00:10:29,180 --> 00:10:30,440
that you see when you look down

263
00:10:30,440 --> 00:10:31,260
on the microscope.

264
00:10:32,020 --> 00:10:33,460
Now, a target cell, basically,

265
00:10:33,680 --> 00:10:35,580
is when there is a bleb of membrane

266
00:10:35,580 --> 00:10:37,860
that results.

267
00:10:38,440 --> 00:10:39,720
And the bleb of membrane

268
00:10:39,720 --> 00:10:42,820
allows for hemoglobin to build up

269
00:10:42,820 --> 00:10:43,520
in that bleb,

270
00:10:43,780 --> 00:10:46,420
which then creates a little bit of redness

271
00:10:46,420 --> 00:10:47,680
within the center of the cell.

272
00:10:47,800 --> 00:10:49,000
And so this is the target cell

273
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:50,600
will then give you this little dot of red

274
00:10:50,600 --> 00:10:52,640
in the center of the area

275
00:10:52,640 --> 00:10:54,160
where there should be central pallor.

276
00:10:54,560 --> 00:10:55,600
So that's a target cell.

277
00:10:56,060 --> 00:10:57,860
Now, when would we see a target cell?

278
00:10:58,040 --> 00:11:00,020
Well, a target cell,

279
00:11:00,460 --> 00:11:01,860
in order to see a target cell,

280
00:11:02,100 --> 00:11:03,780
we need to have either

281
00:11:03,780 --> 00:11:06,460
decrease in the cytoplasm

282
00:11:06,460 --> 00:11:08,280
or increase in the amount of membrane.

283
00:11:08,420 --> 00:11:10,040
Maybe the better way to understand this

284
00:11:10,040 --> 00:11:11,560
would be to think about a basketball.

285
00:11:12,040 --> 00:11:12,900
If you have a basketball

286
00:11:12,900 --> 00:11:15,380
and you press on the surface of the basketball,

287
00:11:15,620 --> 00:11:16,540
you're not going to be able

288
00:11:16,540 --> 00:11:18,040
to indent the surface at all.

289
00:11:18,420 --> 00:11:20,080
However, if you remove some air,

290
00:11:20,540 --> 00:11:22,360
then you can potentially indent the surface

291
00:11:22,360 --> 00:11:24,580
or you could create a bleb

292
00:11:24,580 --> 00:11:26,840
of the surface of the basketball.

293
00:11:27,360 --> 00:11:28,360
So in the same way,

294
00:11:28,480 --> 00:11:30,080
what's happening in thalassemia

295
00:11:30,080 --> 00:11:31,860
is that we're sort of reducing

296
00:11:31,860 --> 00:11:33,940
the hemoglobin that's in the cytoplasm.

297
00:11:33,940 --> 00:11:35,940
And by reducing the hemoglobin

298
00:11:35,940 --> 00:11:36,940
that's in the cytoplasm,

299
00:11:37,020 --> 00:11:38,960
it's like taking the air out of this ball,

300
00:11:39,300 --> 00:11:40,820
which then creates the opportunity

301
00:11:40,820 --> 00:11:43,160
to have a little excess membrane

302
00:11:43,160 --> 00:11:45,480
to be able to produce this bleb

303
00:11:45,480 --> 00:11:46,860
and form a target.

304
00:11:47,480 --> 00:11:49,400
And here's an image of a target cell,

305
00:11:49,540 --> 00:11:51,340
and it's exactly the way I drew it.

306
00:11:51,420 --> 00:11:53,280
Here I'm highlighting the target cell for you.

307
00:11:53,600 --> 00:11:55,100
Normally, this area in the center

308
00:11:55,100 --> 00:11:57,040
should actually be an area of central pallor.

309
00:11:57,360 --> 00:11:58,720
However, in this particular case,

310
00:11:58,920 --> 00:12:01,440
you can clearly see that there is a target

311
00:12:01,440 --> 00:12:02,840
sort of in the center there.

312
00:12:02,840 --> 00:12:05,840
All right, now in this particular disease,

313
00:12:06,720 --> 00:12:08,760
which again is beta thalassemia minor,

314
00:12:09,400 --> 00:12:11,820
the key hemoglobin electrophoresis finding

315
00:12:11,820 --> 00:12:13,680
is that there's going to be usually

316
00:12:13,680 --> 00:12:16,600
an isolated increase in hemoglobin A2.

317
00:12:17,240 --> 00:12:18,380
That's really the key finding.

318
00:12:18,700 --> 00:12:20,260
So I would say when you think about

319
00:12:20,260 --> 00:12:22,240
beta thalassemia major,

320
00:12:22,860 --> 00:12:23,840
it's usually asymptomatic.

321
00:12:24,560 --> 00:12:27,120
The patients have a microcytic hypochromic anemia

322
00:12:27,120 --> 00:12:28,120
with target cells.

323
00:12:28,120 --> 00:12:30,480
And then a key finding is an isolated

324
00:12:30,480 --> 00:12:34,000
or an independent increase in hemoglobin A2.

325
00:12:34,240 --> 00:12:36,760
And that's the key take-home message here.

326
00:12:37,400 --> 00:12:39,140
The other extreme of disease would be

327
00:12:39,140 --> 00:12:41,160
to knock out both beta chains completely,

328
00:12:41,320 --> 00:12:43,080
so that it would be beta null, beta null.

329
00:12:43,400 --> 00:12:46,160
And that's the most severe form of disease.

330
00:12:46,360 --> 00:12:50,360
So it is the opposite extreme of beta thalassemia minor.

331
00:12:50,840 --> 00:12:52,400
So in beta thalassemia major,

332
00:12:52,960 --> 00:12:55,000
both beta chains are knocked out.

333
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,240
And so will there be a problem in the fetus?

334
00:12:57,380 --> 00:12:58,180
The answer is no,

335
00:12:58,280 --> 00:13:02,540
because fetal hemoglobin is actually alpha 2 gamma 2.

336
00:13:02,840 --> 00:13:04,500
And so we don't need beta in the fetus,

337
00:13:04,560 --> 00:13:05,920
and so there's no problem in the fetus.

338
00:13:06,240 --> 00:13:08,080
However, as soon as the baby's born,

339
00:13:08,460 --> 00:13:10,640
there'll be a demand to produce hemoglobin A,

340
00:13:10,940 --> 00:13:13,820
and so these patients will present with severe anemia.

341
00:13:14,220 --> 00:13:16,500
Now it takes a few months for the disease to develop,

342
00:13:16,620 --> 00:13:19,700
and that's because the baby has fetal hemoglobin

343
00:13:19,700 --> 00:13:21,520
floating around in the blood.

344
00:13:21,520 --> 00:13:26,420
And so that fetal hemoglobin is temporarily protected.

345
00:13:26,740 --> 00:13:28,260
So it's going to take a few months to develop.

346
00:13:28,980 --> 00:13:30,620
Now what's going to happen,

347
00:13:30,880 --> 00:13:33,100
because there are no beta chains, correct?

348
00:13:33,220 --> 00:13:34,320
The beta chains are knocked out.

349
00:13:34,580 --> 00:13:36,460
So now as alpha chains are produced,

350
00:13:36,580 --> 00:13:38,120
the alpha chains will dimerize,

351
00:13:38,420 --> 00:13:40,600
and then eventually you'll have alpha 2

352
00:13:40,600 --> 00:13:41,920
looking to join beta 2,

353
00:13:42,040 --> 00:13:43,260
but because there's no beta 2,

354
00:13:43,360 --> 00:13:45,280
eventually alpha 2 will join alpha 2,

355
00:13:45,600 --> 00:13:47,960
which will then produce tetramers of alpha.

356
00:13:47,960 --> 00:13:50,660
And these alpha tetramers will precipitate,

357
00:13:50,980 --> 00:13:52,380
and they'll damage the red blood cell.

358
00:13:52,700 --> 00:13:54,160
When they damage the red blood cell,

359
00:13:54,400 --> 00:13:55,760
that'll result in something called

360
00:13:55,760 --> 00:13:57,420
ineffective erythropoiesis.

361
00:13:57,540 --> 00:13:59,520
What that means is that as the red blood cells

362
00:13:59,520 --> 00:14:02,440
are being made and alpha is being produced,

363
00:14:02,760 --> 00:14:04,640
the alpha will actually precipitate

364
00:14:04,640 --> 00:14:06,100
and damage the red blood cell

365
00:14:06,100 --> 00:14:07,420
as they're being generated.

366
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,100
Another problem is that even if the red blood cell

367
00:14:10,100 --> 00:14:11,980
does manage to get out of the bone marrow,

368
00:14:12,460 --> 00:14:15,160
the spleen will recognize the abnormality

369
00:14:15,160 --> 00:14:17,400
of this precipitated hemoglobin present

370
00:14:17,400 --> 00:14:20,300
within the red blood cell

371
00:14:20,300 --> 00:14:22,800
and will actually destroy the red blood cell

372
00:14:22,800 --> 00:14:25,700
within the splenic macrophages.

373
00:14:26,320 --> 00:14:28,280
And so this is the reason why these patients

374
00:14:28,280 --> 00:14:29,260
have severe anemia.

375
00:14:29,640 --> 00:14:32,320
Now because they have such severe anemia,

376
00:14:32,800 --> 00:14:35,260
they get a massive erythroid hyperplasia.

377
00:14:35,840 --> 00:14:36,860
What do I mean by that?

378
00:14:37,140 --> 00:14:38,280
Well before you can understand

379
00:14:38,280 --> 00:14:39,580
massive erythroid hyperplasia,

380
00:14:39,740 --> 00:14:41,960
you need to recognize and remind yourself

381
00:14:41,960 --> 00:14:46,040
that usually erythropoiesis and hematopoiesis,

382
00:14:46,440 --> 00:14:48,120
in particular erythropoiesis,

383
00:14:48,200 --> 00:14:51,000
occurs usually in the central axial skeleton.

384
00:14:51,160 --> 00:14:52,420
Now when a baby's born,

385
00:14:52,980 --> 00:14:54,540
erythropoiesis and hematopoiesis

386
00:14:54,540 --> 00:14:57,240
will be present throughout most of the skeleton.

387
00:14:57,960 --> 00:14:59,900
But as the patient grows,

388
00:15:00,200 --> 00:15:03,300
usually the erythropoiesis is limited

389
00:15:03,300 --> 00:15:05,920
to the central axial skeleton.

390
00:15:06,840 --> 00:15:07,920
In this particular case,

391
00:15:07,980 --> 00:15:10,040
because there's massive erythroid hyperplasia,

392
00:15:10,040 --> 00:15:11,780
there's such severe anemia

393
00:15:11,780 --> 00:15:13,500
that there's gonna be massive amounts

394
00:15:13,500 --> 00:15:16,000
of erythropoietin being released by the kidney

395
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:17,580
in response to that anemia,

396
00:15:17,780 --> 00:15:19,760
and that massive amounts of erythropoietin

397
00:15:19,760 --> 00:15:21,320
will actually go to the bone marrow

398
00:15:21,320 --> 00:15:23,760
and drive the bone marrow to undergo hyperplasia.

399
00:15:24,160 --> 00:15:27,020
There'll actually be expansion of the hematopoiesis

400
00:15:27,020 --> 00:15:29,540
into unique areas such as the skull

401
00:15:29,540 --> 00:15:30,520
and the facial bones.

402
00:15:31,060 --> 00:15:33,120
Now when there's expansion of hematopoiesis

403
00:15:33,120 --> 00:15:33,860
into the skull,

404
00:15:34,200 --> 00:15:37,080
that's gonna produce a classic crew cut appearance

405
00:15:37,080 --> 00:15:37,720
on x-ray,

406
00:15:37,720 --> 00:15:39,620
and so that's high yield to be familiar with.

407
00:15:40,060 --> 00:15:40,820
And at the same time,

408
00:15:40,900 --> 00:15:42,960
when there's expansion of hematopoiesis

409
00:15:42,960 --> 00:15:44,020
into the facial bones,

410
00:15:44,300 --> 00:15:45,860
that's gonna thicken the facial bones

411
00:15:45,860 --> 00:15:49,080
and give the patient a chipmunk-like face.

412
00:15:49,940 --> 00:15:51,020
What will also occur

413
00:15:51,020 --> 00:15:52,440
is that these patients will get

414
00:15:52,440 --> 00:15:54,420
extramedullary hematopoiesis

415
00:15:54,420 --> 00:15:56,320
so that the liver and spleen

416
00:15:56,320 --> 00:15:59,380
will also begin to produce red blood cells,

417
00:15:59,520 --> 00:16:01,280
and obviously that would be extramedullary,

418
00:16:01,280 --> 00:16:04,940
and that's gonna result in hepatosplenomegaly.

419
00:16:05,720 --> 00:16:08,120
Another complication that can be seen in these patients

420
00:16:08,120 --> 00:16:09,900
is a risk of aplastic crisis

421
00:16:09,900 --> 00:16:11,860
with parvovirus B19 infection.

422
00:16:12,400 --> 00:16:13,300
To understand this,

423
00:16:13,380 --> 00:16:14,540
we have to remind ourselves

424
00:16:14,540 --> 00:16:16,360
that these patients are dependent

425
00:16:16,360 --> 00:16:18,640
on every single red blood cell

426
00:16:18,640 --> 00:16:20,100
that's produced by the bone marrow

427
00:16:20,100 --> 00:16:23,080
along with extramedullary hematopoiesis.

428
00:16:23,520 --> 00:16:25,060
Now parvovirus B19

429
00:16:25,060 --> 00:16:28,240
is a virus that infects erythroid precursors,

430
00:16:28,240 --> 00:16:30,860
and when it infects erythroid precursors,

431
00:16:31,140 --> 00:16:32,220
it shuts them down.

432
00:16:32,820 --> 00:16:35,640
Normally, it's a self-limited infection,

433
00:16:35,860 --> 00:16:38,580
and it'll last for a week or two weeks,

434
00:16:38,980 --> 00:16:41,400
and in most patients, in the normal patient,

435
00:16:41,520 --> 00:16:43,020
it wouldn't really have any effect

436
00:16:43,020 --> 00:16:44,680
because, for example, you and I

437
00:16:44,680 --> 00:16:47,120
have a tremendous reserve in our bone marrow,

438
00:16:47,240 --> 00:16:49,580
and we can afford to shut down

439
00:16:49,580 --> 00:16:52,360
production of red blood cells for a week or two.

440
00:16:52,680 --> 00:16:53,920
However, these patients,

441
00:16:54,080 --> 00:16:55,860
the beta-thalassemia major patients,

442
00:16:55,860 --> 00:16:57,800
are so dependent on every single

443
00:16:57,800 --> 00:16:59,300
red blood cell that they produce

444
00:16:59,300 --> 00:17:02,480
that they can't risk shut down even for a day.

445
00:17:03,100 --> 00:17:05,740
And so parvovirus B19 in these patients

446
00:17:05,740 --> 00:17:07,860
would create an aplastic crisis,

447
00:17:08,460 --> 00:17:10,000
and so that's an important thing

448
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:10,860
to be familiar with.

449
00:17:11,340 --> 00:17:12,340
Here's a classic image

450
00:17:12,340 --> 00:17:13,860
that shows up on examinations.

451
00:17:14,100 --> 00:17:16,460
This is the crew-cut appearance of the skull,

452
00:17:16,840 --> 00:17:17,820
and again, it's indicative

453
00:17:17,820 --> 00:17:21,740
of massive expansion of hematopoiesis

454
00:17:21,740 --> 00:17:24,500
into regions where hematopoiesis

455
00:17:24,500 --> 00:17:26,740
would not normally occur within the bone.

456
00:17:28,200 --> 00:17:30,060
It's not uncommon for these patients

457
00:17:30,060 --> 00:17:32,340
to be dependent on transfusions,

458
00:17:32,480 --> 00:17:34,620
and so chronic transfusions are often necessary.

459
00:17:35,160 --> 00:17:36,660
Now, this will lead to a risk

460
00:17:36,660 --> 00:17:38,260
for secondary hemochromatosis,

461
00:17:38,440 --> 00:17:39,940
and what do we mean by that?

462
00:17:40,360 --> 00:17:41,680
Well, basically, remember that

463
00:17:41,680 --> 00:17:43,460
every time you're giving a bag of blood,

464
00:17:43,560 --> 00:17:45,100
you're actually giving a bag of iron

465
00:17:45,100 --> 00:17:46,360
because those red blood cells

466
00:17:46,360 --> 00:17:47,360
are going to be broken down,

467
00:17:47,660 --> 00:17:49,400
and that iron is now going to be present

468
00:17:49,400 --> 00:17:50,120
within the body.

469
00:17:50,260 --> 00:17:52,600
Remember that the body really has no way

470
00:17:52,600 --> 00:17:53,480
of getting rid of iron,

471
00:17:53,720 --> 00:17:56,760
so every bag of blood is basically a bag of iron,

472
00:17:57,060 --> 00:17:59,420
and as iron begins to build up within the tissues,

473
00:17:59,800 --> 00:18:01,260
eventually the complication

474
00:18:01,260 --> 00:18:03,520
of having excess iron in tissues

475
00:18:03,520 --> 00:18:05,320
is called hemochromatosis,

476
00:18:05,800 --> 00:18:07,600
and that's a disease we'll talk about later

477
00:18:07,600 --> 00:18:09,760
in the liver lectures.

478
00:18:10,140 --> 00:18:11,080
However, at this point,

479
00:18:11,160 --> 00:18:12,660
you should recognize that it's secondary

480
00:18:12,660 --> 00:18:16,240
because it's secondary due to chronic transfusions.

481
00:18:17,260 --> 00:18:18,160
On blood smear,

482
00:18:18,400 --> 00:18:19,240
these patients will have

483
00:18:19,240 --> 00:18:21,900
a microcytic hypochromic anemia

484
00:18:21,900 --> 00:18:23,120
with target cells,

485
00:18:23,200 --> 00:18:24,500
and I've already explained that.

486
00:18:24,780 --> 00:18:25,480
At the same time,

487
00:18:25,540 --> 00:18:27,360
there will be some nucleated red blood cells.

488
00:18:27,740 --> 00:18:28,320
Now, normally,

489
00:18:28,440 --> 00:18:30,160
our red blood cells do not have nuclei,

490
00:18:30,600 --> 00:18:32,500
and we remove those nuclei

491
00:18:32,500 --> 00:18:33,680
before the red blood cells

492
00:18:33,680 --> 00:18:35,540
can go out into the blood.

493
00:18:36,060 --> 00:18:37,580
However, if red blood cells

494
00:18:37,580 --> 00:18:39,540
are made in an abnormal location,

495
00:18:39,740 --> 00:18:41,220
for example, the spleen or the liver,

496
00:18:41,360 --> 00:18:42,640
as would occur in this disease,

497
00:18:43,020 --> 00:18:44,800
some of the nucleated red blood cells

498
00:18:44,800 --> 00:18:46,940
can actually escape out into the blood,

499
00:18:46,940 --> 00:18:50,400
and so we might see circulating nucleated red blood cells

500
00:18:50,400 --> 00:18:51,980
in this particular disease.

501
00:18:52,600 --> 00:18:53,540
On electrophoresis,

502
00:18:53,620 --> 00:18:54,340
the key finding,

503
00:18:54,640 --> 00:18:55,660
no hemoglobin A.

504
00:18:56,260 --> 00:18:58,240
The only hemoglobins that the patient will have

505
00:18:58,240 --> 00:18:59,300
are hemoglobin A2,

506
00:18:59,660 --> 00:19:00,040
and again,

507
00:19:00,120 --> 00:19:04,340
A2 is made up of alpha 2 and delta 2,

508
00:19:04,660 --> 00:19:06,680
and so there's no beta needed to make A2,

509
00:19:06,840 --> 00:19:08,240
and they'll have hemoglobin F,

510
00:19:08,280 --> 00:19:11,720
and hemoglobin F is alpha 2 and gamma 2,

511
00:19:11,720 --> 00:19:12,440
and again,

512
00:19:12,580 --> 00:19:13,580
there's no need for beta,

513
00:19:13,740 --> 00:19:17,100
so the key finding would be no hemoglobin A.

514
00:19:17,820 --> 00:19:19,220
And that concludes our discussion

515
00:19:19,220 --> 00:19:21,380
of microcytic anemias.

516
00:19:21,820 --> 00:19:25,000
The next section will deal with macrocytic anemia.

